Since Java 8, the feature gap between abstract class and interface has narrowed significantly. Let's compare every aspect:

```java
// ═══ INTERFACE (Java 8+) ═══
public interface PaymentChannel {
    // Abstract method — implementor MUST provide body
    TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount);

    // Default method (Java 8) — has body, inherited by implementors
    default boolean isAvailable() {
        return true;  // default: always available. Implementors CAN override.
    }

    // Static method (Java 8) — utility, called via InterfaceName.method()
    static PaymentChannel defaultChannel() {
        return new NeftChannel();
    }

    // Private method (Java 9) — helper for default methods
    private void logTransfer(String msg) {
        System.out.println("[PaymentChannel] " + msg);
    }

    // Constants only — no instance fields!
    BigDecimal MAX_TRANSFER = new BigDecimal("10000000");  // implicitly public static final
    // BigDecimal balance;  // ❌ COMPILE ERROR: interfaces cannot have instance fields!
}

// ═══ ABSTRACT CLASS ═══
public abstract class Account {
    // INSTANCE FIELDS — the key advantage over interface!
    private final String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;  // shared mutable state
    private final List<String> auditLog = new ArrayList<>();

    // CONSTRUCTOR — initializes shared state
    protected Account(String accountId, BigDecimal initialBalance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
        auditLog.add("OPENED: Rs." + initialBalance);
    }

    // CONCRETE method — shared implementation, NOT abstract
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        auditLog.add("CREDIT: Rs." + amount);
    }

    // ABSTRACT method — subclass MUST implement
    public abstract BigDecimal calculateInterest();

    // Template Method Pattern: concrete method calling abstract method
    public void processMonthEnd() {
        BigDecimal interest = calculateInterest();  // calls subclass implementation!
        if (interest.signum() > 0) {
            deposit(interest);
            auditLog.add("INTEREST: Rs." + interest);
        }
    }

    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    public List<String> getAuditLog() { return Collections.unmodifiableList(auditLog); }
}
```

WHEN does the choice ACTUALLY matter? Five decision rules:

```java
// RULE 1: Need SHARED MUTABLE STATE → abstract class
// Interface: only public static final constants. No instance fields.
// Abstract class: can have private/protected fields, constructors, state.
abstract class Account {
    protected BigDecimal balance;        // shared state across all subclasses
    protected Account(BigDecimal bal) { this.balance = bal; }
}
// Interface CANNOT do this. If you need shared fields → abstract class.

// RULE 2: Need MULTIPLE INHERITANCE of behavior → interface
// Java: single class inheritance, multiple interface implementation.
class SavingsAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Notifiable, Transferable {
    // Extends ONE class. Implements MULTIPLE interfaces.
}
// Cannot: class X extends Account, AnotherClass { }  // ❌ COMPILE ERROR

// RULE 3: Defining a CAPABILITY (adjective) → interface
// "Can be serialized" → Serializable (interface)
// "Can be compared" → Comparable (interface)
// "Can be audited" → Auditable (interface)
// Capabilities are CROSS-CUTTING — many unrelated classes may have them.

// RULE 4: Defining a TYPE HIERARCHY (noun) → abstract class
// "Is an Account" → Account (abstract class)
// Savings IS-A Account. Current IS-A Account.
// Type hierarchy has shared state + partial implementation.

// RULE 5: MODERN DEFAULT — prefer interface
// If you don't need shared mutable state → interface with default methods.
// Reason: class can implement MULTIPLE interfaces but extend only ONE class.
// Don't "waste" the single inheritance slot on something an interface could do.
```

Real-world comparison in Spring:

```java
// Spring uses INTERFACES everywhere:
public interface JpaRepository<T, ID> extends ListCrudRepository<T, ID> { }
// WHY: classes can implement multiple repositories. No shared state needed.

// Spring's AbstractController (abstract class — now deprecated):
public abstract class AbstractController {
    private WebApplicationContext context;  // shared state!
    protected abstract ModelAndView handleRequest(...);
}
// WHY abstract class: shared state (context) + template method (handleRequest).
```

